# Monorepo Cursor Rules

## Project Structure
- This is a monorepo with multiple packages under `/packages/`
- Each package may have different technologies and patterns
- Always maintain consistent patterns within each package
- Use relative imports within packages, absolute imports with aliases when available

## General Principles
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Write self-documenting code with clear naming
- Follow SOLID principles
- Prefer immutable data patterns
- Write code that is easy to test and maintain
- Use TypeScript strictly - avoid `any` types
- Prefer explicit over implicit when it improves clarity

---

# Angular Package Rules (`packages/angular-client-app/`)

## Architecture Patterns

### Component Structure
- Use **standalone components** for new components when possible
- Mix with NgModules only when necessary for complex lazy loading
- Follow feature-based folder structure: `/features/{feature-name}/components/{component-name}/`
- Each component folder should contain: `*.component.ts`, `*.component.html`, `*.component.scss`, `*.component.spec.ts`, `index.ts`
- Export components from `index.ts` for clean imports

### Component Best Practices
```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'feature-component-name', // Use feature prefix
  imports: [CommonModule, /* other standalone components */],
  templateUrl: './component.component.html',
  styleUrl: './component.component.scss',
  encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom, // Prefer ShadowDom for isolation
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush, // Always use OnPush
})
```

### State Management
- Use Angular signals for reactive state management
- Store services should be injectable with `providedIn: 'root'`
- Use `signal()` for mutable state and `computed()` for derived state
- Update signals immutably using `.update()` method
- Akita is available but prefer Angular signals for new code

### Services
- All services should use `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })`
- Use dependency injection via `inject()` function in components and services
- Services should be placed in appropriate feature folders or `/core/services/`
- Use constructor injection only when necessary (legacy compatibility)

### Types and Interfaces
- Define all types in `types.ts` files within feature folders
- Use PascalCase with `I` prefix for interfaces (e.g., `ICell`, `IWorkbook`)
- Export type aliases for commonly used types (e.g., `type CellValue = ICell['value']`)
- Use `Record<string, T>` for key-value mappings

### Routing
- Use lazy loading with `loadChildren` for feature modules
- Feature routes should be defined in `{feature}.route.ts` files
- Main routes in `app.routes.ts` should delegate to feature routes

### Styling
- Use SCSS for all styling
- Component styles should be scoped using ShadowDom encapsulation
- Global styles go in `src/styles.scss`
- Follow CSS naming conventions (kebab-case for classes)

### Testing
- Use Jest with jest-preset-angular
- Test files should have `.spec.ts` extension
- Write unit tests for all components, services, and pipes
- Mock external dependencies in tests
- Use path mapping `@core/*` for imports in tests

### File Organization
```
src/app/
├── core/                    # Core application services and configuration
│   ├── services/           # Global services
│   └── core.config.ts      # Core providers configuration
├── features/               # Feature modules
│   └── {feature-name}/
│       ├── components/     # Feature components
│       ├── store/         # Feature state management
│       ├── types.ts       # Feature type definitions
│       ├── {feature}.module.ts
│       └── {feature}.route.ts
├── shared/                 # Shared utilities and types
│   └── types/             # Global type definitions
├── app.component.*        # Root component
├── app.config.ts          # Application configuration
└── app.routes.ts          # Main routing configuration
```

### Import Patterns
- Use path mapping with `@core/*` alias
- Import Angular modules from `@angular/core`, `@angular/common`, etc.
- Import shared components using relative paths within features
- Use index.ts files for clean exports

### Event Handling
- Use proper TypeScript typing for events
- Emit custom events using `@Output() eventName = new EventEmitter<T>()`
- Handle DOM events with proper event typing
- Add and remove event listeners in lifecycle hooks (ngOnDestroy)

### Dependency Injection
- Use `inject()` function for dependency injection in new code
- Inject common services like `DOCUMENT`, `ElementRef` when needed
- Use `@ViewChild()` for template references with proper typing

### Code Style
- Use single quotes for strings
- Prefer template literals for complex strings
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`)
- Destructure objects and arrays when appropriate
- Use meaningful variable and function names

### Performance
- Always use `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`
- Use `ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom` for style isolation
- Avoid memory leaks by cleaning up subscriptions and event listeners
- Use `setTimeout()` for DOM updates that require view rendering

### Angular Specific Rules
- Angular version: 19.x
- Use SSR capabilities when beneficial
- Prefer reactive programming with signals over RxJS when possible
- Use standalone components as the default approach
- Follow Angular style guide conventions

## Common Patterns to Follow

### Signal-based Reactive State
```typescript
export class MyStore {
  private _data = signal<MyData[]>([]);
  readonly data = this._data.asReadonly();
  
  updateData(newData: MyData) {
    this._data.update(current => [...current, newData]);
  }
}
```

### Component with Proper Lifecycle Management
```typescript
export class MyComponent implements OnDestroy {
  private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();
  
  ngOnDestroy() {
    this.destroy$.next();
    this.destroy$.complete();
  }
}
```

### Proper Event Listener Management
```typescript
export class MyComponent implements OnDestroy {
  private eventListeners: (() => void)[] = [];
  
  private addEventListeners() {
    const listener = () => { /* handler */ };
    document.addEventListener('event', listener);
    this.eventListeners.push(() => document.removeEventListener('event', listener));
  }
  
  ngOnDestroy() {
    this.eventListeners.forEach(cleanup => cleanup());
  }
}
```

## Code Generation Guidelines
- Always include proper TypeScript types
- Add necessary imports automatically
- Follow the established folder structure
- Include basic tests for new components/services
- Use the existing configuration and setup patterns
- Maintain consistency with existing code style and patterns 